    "insert": "The study aims to improve detection of cervical cancer by increasing the resolution of Pap Smears through the use of Enhanced Super-resolution General Adversarial Networks (ESRGAN). The pictures taken of cervical cells are of poor quality due to many factors, by using an ESRGAN, these factors could be eliminated.\nThe state-of-the-art (SOTA) explains where reasearch in the field of interest is at the moment and where the study lies within this field. Your SOTA clearly explains where the medical field stands at the moment, and what problems current researchers trying to improve imagery are facing. The link to your study is clear. \nour Theoretical Framework and Methodology show you have a clear understanding of how and why the application of ESRGANs may benefit the detection of cervical cancer, and the step-by-step Methodology is easy to follow and understand.\nPointing out Challenges and providing possible solutions shows you have adequate knowledge on the subject. The Motivation is well structured and captures interest, however, it can seem unprofessional to mention which part of the Motivation each of the text passages is. Which of them is Hook, Anker and Teaser is only relevant while you write the text, you may leave the captions out in the final work.\nWhile you correctly understand that ESRGANs may be hard to implement, asking for advice in online frums may not be a feasible source of information. People may post incorrect or outright misleading Information, with no way of confirming their validity. It is better to try and contact professionals in the Field, as they have quantifiable knowledge on the subject.\nYou should change the end of your schedule to include a few weeks of buffer time. As studies are unpredictable, you may find yourself in a situation where you need to re-run tests or rewrite a large portion of your study, and if this happens towards the end of your schedule, you might run out of time. Therfore it is best practise to plan for such setbacks by including buffer time right at the end.\nThere were minor grammatical errors in your text, that, while not making the text unreadable, can lead to confusion when reading the text for the first time. I suggest using a grammar tool to find and correct such errors before publishing. \nOverall, while containing minor formal errors, your study is well-written and easily understood.\n"
